A||N||24||Monkeyman||Who's done the GA/Beretta brake upgrade???||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||09:48 PM||||I plan on doing this myself (only because I'm stupid [read:mechanically challenged and wanting to work on brakes] and there's no one else around willing to anything other than stock work) and I have some questions.  <P>The way I understand from reading Randy Agees' instructions, the rears are a direct bolt on (zero modifications), but you have to do a bunch of modifications to the front.  I don't see any major problems with removing and replacing with new, but he said something about having to grind off part of the mounting bracket for the calipers (3mm?).  How would I go about doing that if I don't have a grinder (all I have is a Dremel and a couple of drills)?  And how should I make sure I get 1) exactly 3mm ground off, and 2) keep them flat (as opposed to an angle)?  I'd like to do this before I head to Ohio next month.<P>Can anyone help???<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||07-09-2000||09:58 PM||||MM, You can get a grinding stone for the dremel tool and just grind the spots until the rotor doesn't rub then grind a little more for clearence. The measurement doesn't have to be exact. You do need 1/32 of an inch clearence between the rotor and the spots you grind down.<BR>If you want the link below is to a website that has done all the work making the hubs.<BR>For $110.00 he sends you the ground hubs, bearings, seals, and new studs! If  rumors are true where it was costing $150 to grind down the rotors you'd save a few bucks. Check out the link. (I'm doing it this winter) <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/_XOOM/jrisagod/brakekit1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/_XOOM/jrisagod/brakekit1.htm</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||07-09-2000||10:13 PM||||I just did the shu-bang a couple months ago.  I borrowed a small grinder from a friend, and it still took me along time to grind down that metal.  IMO, a dremmel would take forever, but you do with what you have (it gets pretty loud in a garage with a grinder, neighbors come over and check out what you are doing).  <P>I can vouch for JR and his hubs, he's also pretty good about answering questions if you call him directly.  He might be a little pricey, (other guys here can do it), but the Fiero doesn't have to be down.  I personally had a little trouble with mine, and took 2 weeks to do (taking my time, still had another car to drive).  I did have to go with the Blazer MC because I was almost to the floor before the brakes were really grabing.  <P>Just make sure you either have a long weekend to do it, or another car to drive while doing the job.  <P>BTW, don't forget to paint the calipers, they start rusting on you exactly one month after installing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||10:21 PM||||Thanx, Oreif.  I can get the machine work done for around $50, but then I'd still have to pay for the parts and find someone to remove the old studs and replace them with new.<P>In Randy Agees article, he says that I should take the new rotors with me to the machine shop so they can be sure to machine the old rotors to the right size.  Something about not wanting too much play?<P>What are bearing races?  And what exactly will I have to do once I get this guys package?  Do I just slap the new rotor assys on (after the grinding--this is the scary part) and go down the road?<P>Anyone want to come over to Indiana and do the work for me?  (I think I'm kidding.)<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||10:29 PM||||BBBAD GT--A long weekend off?  Right.  I've had exactly 3 days off in the last 4 months.  On the other hand, I only live about 8 blocks from work and I need the exercise.<P>What kind of trouble did you have?  I don't have time for a bunch of problems.  Somehow I need to get all of this done by the 12th of August for a trip.<P>So, what I'll need (other than this guys kit) is: (4) caliper, (4) rotors, brake pads, a MC, some grease, paint and time?  If that's all I'll need, I can probably save about $20 with his kit.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000005||cifc||07-09-2000||10:31 PM||||I just did this last month on my 87 and am in the middle of doing it to my 85.  I used a dremel to to the grinding only took about 10 mins a side.  I was lucky and got the rotors machined for free.  I also used the blazer mc.  I've been really happy with the results.<BR>--Jason||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||10:41 PM||||Well.  Looks like this will be my next project for the Grape Ape.  The current brakes are simply scary.  (Yes, it stops, but usually with an "Oh, crap.  Get out of the way." feeling.)  I'm still a little nervous about doing this since I really am mechanically challenged, but since no one in my area is willing to do anything that isn't totally stock, I don't seem to have much choice.<P>How long do you guys figure it should take per side (front) to do this?  I have to do this outside (can't get in the garage yet) and don't want to do it in the rain (it's rained almost everyday for about 3 weeks now--drought my butt).<P>Do I have to grind anything down to do the rears?  I'm under the impression that the rears require no more than a screwdriver and a socket set?<P>You guys/gals are the best.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000007||cifc||07-09-2000||10:45 PM||||About all you have to do on the rears is stash the e-brake cable and bend back the dust shield.  Make sure to bend it clear.  Mine rubbed really bad a first, but it was easy to fix.<BR>--Jason||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||10:53 PM||||The E-brake cables have been in the trash for a while.  How much do I need to bend the dust shields back?  Will they get tangled up in the suspension if I bend them back too far?  (I've never looked at the rear.)<P>Thanx again for the help.  Keep it coming.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000009||cifc||07-09-2000||11:01 PM||||Just bend them far enough so they clear the rotor.  If they start to rub you can probably bend them back without removinr the wheel.  I could, but my wheels are pretty open.  Stock ones might be difficult.  I don't think you'll have to worry about them getting tangled in anythin.  I think I'm just going to remove them on the 85.  I'll have to look at it again.<BR>--Jason||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||07-09-2000||11:35 PM||||MM- I've 2 new rotors for the project. I bought them to convert my 85, but sold the car before upgrading the brakes. I will sell them cheap if you or anyone else wants them. $15.00 for both?||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||07-09-2000||11:42 PM||||I'm sure you have looked into parts already, but I spent just under $200 in 4 rotors and 4 loaded calipers from carparts.com with the 33% off deal (with core charges, wasn't worth sending the cores back).  Parts came fairly quickly.  I went with all Bendix stuff, to keep it consistant.  <P>BTW Dave, remember when I said my brakes before the swap were horrible?   <P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||07-10-2000||12:04 AM||||Monkey, there are two minor modifications you have to do for each corner, front and rear. The first is to bend the dust shield back to clear the thicker rotors and to trim it a little in a couple spots. The article has a pic. The second thing is to grind down the caliper mounting pad a bit so the new calipers will fit into place. I used a 4 inch hand grinder which took very little time. I had to do this to both front and rear.<P>I was like you, I know how to do brakes, but was nervous about messing something up. Reading the instructions makes it sound harder than it is. Once you start fitting everything together, it'll be obvious what needs to be done. Grind just enough down to fit the caliper on, it's not rocket science. Bend the shields back a little so they don't rub on the rotors.<P>Monkey, I don't know who's doing your hubs, but if you let Greendano do them, you won't have to worry because they'll be perfect. Lug studs are easy to do. Beat them out with a big hammer and then drive the new ones back in with the same BFH. No biggie. Bearing races are what the bearings ride in. When you buy new wheel bearings, the package will include a new race. Put the big hammer away for this job, you don't want anything damaged.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FIEROCIOUS||07-10-2000||05:00 AM||||Monkeyman, I did the upgrade about a year ago and to tell you the truth it took less time to modify and fit the new brakes than it did to do the pads on the original breaks! However I am mechanicaly minded. Just get all the machine work done first then pick a day (thats all you need) and do it!<BR>One note: don't worry about the longer studs Randy in retrospect agreed with me after I e-mailed him. Not worth the hastle of finding them. As long as you have the same amount of thread as the lugnut is wide it's enough thread ie. 3/4" threaded.||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||07-10-2000||07:31 AM||||Yes, I put new lug studs in, but used the factory sized ones for Fieros, no problems. What I did was the front first, made sure they worked and then on another day, I did the rear. That way I didn't have the car down for a long period of time (unlike with all the suspension mods).<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||07-10-2000||12:09 PM||||Cooter--What year and model are the rotors for?<P>Bryan--How does Greendano fit into all of this?  Is he doing the same mod for people?<P>I can basically picture how most of this stuff goes together.  I don't really have a problem swapping one part for another, it's just the fabrication/modification that scares me.  You know...what happens if I grind too much or something?  I can barely afford this let alone afford another set of spindles, etc.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000016||dogtired||07-10-2000||02:53 PM||||This project is a MAYBE???<BR>Is it a thought to use parts off of a Baretta, 87,88 ?? I have a donar without a title, Does it have all the parts I need???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Or do you think I should buy all new??<P>The Master Cylinder from an S10, what year do you use? Could I use it without doing the rest of the Mod??? Or would there be any point to it??<P>Just thinking out loud, Thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000017||Monkeyman||07-10-2000||03:04 PM||||Dog--The way I understand it, you can use any Beretta or Grand Am up through '89.  And you only have 1/2 the parts needed.  The Beretta/GA had front disc brakes, but rear drums.  As far as using your donor car parts compared to new...I guess it all depends what kind of shape the donor parts are.  I would think that the rotors and calipers should be alright as long as there's still enough metal left on the rotors to turn them.  Rotors are pretty cheap, though.  About $15/each, same for the calipers.  If your calipers are junk, you could always use them for the core.<P>I get to start on this Friday.  I gotta figure out who's gonna do what and where I'll be getting parts by then.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfixit58||07-10-2000||04:03 PM||||Hey Dog,<P>The master cylinder you need is off of a FULL size Blazer... NOT the S10. Check out Randy Agees Procedure:<BR> <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/frontpage/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/frontpage/index.html</A> <P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||07-10-2000||11:20 PM||||MM, You can knock out the old wheel studs with a hammer. couple whacks and out they pop. no big deal. <P>Draw the new ones in with a flipped lug nut and a some washers or a bigger nut under it. just grease up the lugnut so it pulls easy. It'll draw them right in. no need for a press. You want to draw the studs with the nut flipped so flat top is doing the pulling. It'll turn easier. Install them just like you'd tighten the wheel, in a star patern. (I'm expecting you're pulling them thru both the hub and rotor at the same time.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||07-11-2000||08:55 PM||||MM, I hate to speak for Dan-o, but he turned down my rotors into hubs for me and they are great. He has access to a metal lathe, I believe. I've seen him post a time or two offering to do this for other members in the past too. Might try emailing him.<P>Here's what I did when I was using the grinder. I'd grind a little bit, then try fitting the caliper. If it didn't slide into place, I'd grind just a little more and try fitting it again, etc., etc., until they fit on like the originals. Take it slow and easy.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Oreif||07-12-2000||02:08 AM||||The Fiero Store now has a brake upgrade kit for fieros! It is bigger vented rotors, Better calipers, And specially made hubs for the front. Total cost is $289.00 There is no special grinding or machining. It is a direct bolt-on kit. Everything is included.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||07-12-2000||12:51 PM||||BB--Thanx a ton, but I guess I'll have to go with the Fiero Stores upgrade kit/package.  Wish I'd known about it before I started this thread.  Then I wouldn't have had to waste everyones time.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000023||cifc||07-12-2000||04:24 PM||||Isn't the Fiero Store kit just all the parts for the front GA upgrade put together in a package deal?||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||07-12-2000||11:01 PM||||If I understand correctly, it's all the right parts, <B>PLUS</B> all the machine work is done and it includes a new caliper bracket thing so you don't have to grind anything off of your car.  I think it's just a matter of opening the package, unbolting stuff from your car and bolting on the new FS stuff.  (At least I hope so.)<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
